#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115248 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115077} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6f890b-fab1-51f1-9ac7-b680553c835a" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186660386}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Matrix Product That's Equivalent To a Sequence of Row Operations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672491} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523120} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186580307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672487}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z &=1, \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4z &=1 \\\\ y+3z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1-4z, \\\\ y &=-3z, \\\\ z &=z.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3eff050b-e4ba-52e5-aa10-7bba2feef2fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494 :tutorial/content "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "e0c85152-daea-5a87-a3ba-81870558c7a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3163 :tutorial/title "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116497 :tutorial/content "To compute the product of an elementary matrix and another matrix, one option is to perform the matrix multiplication as usual. However, there's an easier, quicker way.\n\nRemember that every elementary matrix is the result of applying an elementary row operation to the identity matrix. If we multiply another matrix by an elementary matrix, we're effectively applying the elementary row operation to the other matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we are asked to find the product $EA$ where\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]} \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4} \\\\ {\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6}\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe elementary matrix $E$ comes from swapping the two rows of the identity matrix ($R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$). So, to compute the product $EA,$ all we have to do is swap the two rows of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n$$\nEA = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{blue}8} & {\\color{blue}6} \\\\ {\\color{red}3} & {\\color{red}4}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThis may seem almost too easy. But it's true! And we can verify it by computing the product the long way, using matrix multiplication. Indeed, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}EA & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we say that $E \\cdot A$ is equivalent to applying to $A$ the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to multiply $A$ from the *left*. If we multiply $A$ by an elementary matrix from the *right*, we will perform a *column* operation on $A$ (not a row operation). In this case, multiplying $A$ by $E$ from the right will have the effect of switching the *columns* of $A,$ not the rows.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AE & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "527d3bb7-567f-5115-ad1c-31bd81fe1c23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3166 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119592} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b022adb-2663-550f-866d-3365bb2d78c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186591703}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Product"}] [{:db/i